## Why This Is Asked

This question tests expertise with closures, per-id memoization, and real-time rendering patterns in frontend systems. It specifically targets race-condition handling and efficient UI updates under high-frequency data scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Closure-scoped per-id caches for memory efficiency
- Per-id memoization with bounded history management
- Promise chaining to serialize renders and handle race conditions
- Debounce using requestAnimationFrame for smooth UI updates

## Code Example

```javascript
function createDataUpdater() {
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> [last 5 results]
  let renderPromise = Promise.resolve();
  let renderScheduled = false;

  const updateCache = (id, data) => {
    const history = cache.get(id) || [];
    history.push(data);
    if (history.length > 5) history.shift();
    cache.set(id, history);
  };

  const render = () => {
    renderScheduled = false;
    // Apply cache data to UI
  };

  const scheduleRender = () => {
    if (!renderScheduled) {
      renderScheduled = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(render);
    }
  };

  return {
    processBatch: (batch) => {
      renderPromise = renderPromise.then(() => {
        batch.forEach(item => updateCache(item.id, item.data));
        scheduleRender();
      });
    }
  };
}
```

## Trade-offs

**Pros:**
- Closure ensures encapsulation and prevents external cache mutation
- Bounded cache prevents memory leaks from unbounded growth
- Promise chain guarantees render ordering consistency
- requestAnimationFrame provides optimal browser rendering timing

**Cons:**
- Added complexity compared to simple direct updates
- Memory overhead from maintaining per-id histories
- Potential render lag if batches arrive faster than processing capacity
- Cache management adds computational overhead per item

For optimistic updates, you separate the optimistic UI state from the actual server state. Here's a useReducer implementation:

```javascript
const initialState = {
  data: null,
  status: 'idle', // 'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'
  previousData: null,
  error: null
};

function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE':
      return {
        ...state,
        previousData: state.data,
        data: action.payload,
        status: 'pending'
      };
    case 'SUCCESS':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'success',
        previousData: null
      };
    case 'ERROR':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'error',
        data: state.previousData, // rollback
        previousData: null,
        error: action.error
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}

// Usage in component:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  dispatch({ type: 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE', payload: newData });
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    dispatch({ type: 'SUCCESS' });
  } catch (error) {
    dispatch({ type: 'ERROR', error });
  }
};
```

The useState approach uses useEffect for the server call:

```javascript
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const previousData = data;
  setData(newData); // optimistic update
  setIsPending(true);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
  } catch (error) {
    setData(previousData); // rollback
    setError(error);
  } finally {
    setIsPending(false);
  }
};
```

For race conditions with multiple rapid updates, use AbortController to cancel in-flight requests:

```javascript
const abortRef = useRef(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  // Cancel any in-flight request
  abortRef.current?.abort();

  const controller = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = controller;

  setData(newData); // optimistic
  try {
    await api.update(newData, { signal: controller.signal });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
      setData(previousData);
    }
  }
};
```

Alternatively, use version/sequence tracking to ignore stale responses:

```javascript
const versionRef = useRef(0);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const currentVersion = ++versionRef.current;
  setData(newData);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return; // stale response
    // process success
  } catch (error) {
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return;
    setData(previousData); // rollback
  }
};
```

Key strategies: 1) Store previous state before optimistic update, 2) Use AbortController to cancel stale requests, 3) Track request version to ignore outdated responses, 4) Consider a queue for sequential processing of dependent updates.

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to integrate fetch, WebSocket, and Service Worker workflows in a realistic UI pattern. It emphasizes error handling, reconnection strategies, offline behavior, and efficient DOM updates—critical in production frontend apps.

Key Concepts
- Fetch with AbortController: timeout and robust error handling
- WebSocket lifecycle: onopen/onmessage/onclose, exponential backoff reconnect
- Service Worker: caching shell and API responses; offline/online transitions
- Offline queue: persisting submissions (IndexedDB) and retry logic
- DOM rendering: debounced updates to minimize reflows

Code Example
```javascript
async function loadComments(productId){
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 5000);
  try{
    const res = await fetch(`/api/comments?productId=${productId}`, {signal: ac.signal});
    if(!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status);
    return await res.json();
  } catch(e){
    return [];
  } finally{ clearTimeout(timer); }
}
```

